{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791600013,"nonce":"PmA/JccQzqXYp/h7ePcyR5Og5GI=","message":"Deferral is back on for closed programs. What went wrong with it on, found on the Mac with both reproducers (function_as_value.py printed an empty line for \"aa\", operator_functools.py exited 139, with and without ZYPY_LLVM):\n\n- Release facts. A library function whose recorded facts do not serve the program (those calling a hook the program supplies: zb_call_1, zb_call_2, zb_list_reduce, zb_list_map1 and ~15 more for these two programs) had its facts computed from the shell, with no body, so its callers released what it returns. The runtime now decodes those bodies once lowering is done (decode_bodies_facts_read); the rest stay encoded. Both reproducers then match their expected output in both modes.\n- Addresses. Lua's library bodies take addresses (zl_lib_table, zl_lib_string, zl_pairs, zl_string_gmatch, zl_utf8_codes, ...), which address_taken_functions could not see in a shell. Shells now record what their body refers to (ShellRefs: addresses taken, names called, globals read), and the analysis reads it. Python's library takes none.\n- Literals. With deferral every private library literal was adopted (Lua fib: 2704 globals against 206), making Lua start-up slower than without deferral. A shell's recorded globals now bring its literals as an eager body's do.\n- String-boxing wrappers (zb_box_str) are recorded in the release facts, so a shell keeps the box-adopting rewrite.\n- The inliner classified a bodyless callee as an empty multi-block one; it now leaves the call.\n- Two runtimes in one process: the interpreter's body source for a finished body went through the process-wide lazy optimiser, which another runtime replaces or clears; it now falls back to the body's snapshot source as tier_body and the LLVM source already did (zyntax_python reach_lowering, two test threads, \"missing block\"). The wider callback sharing is 6f87d95.\n\nStart-up instructions (run stopped once start-up is done, ZYxx_PROFILE_STARTUP=10, median of 3, Apple M-series), deferral against 8b08da4a:\n- Lua, 17 benchmarks: 33.70G -\u003e 29.67G (-12%); fib -28%, inlined_call -27%, small kernels -22 to -28%, nbody -7%.\n- Python, 22 kernels: 142.10G -\u003e 137.19G (-3.5%); ai -11%, fannkuch/float/json_bench -8%, go -3.5% (nqueens varies +-8% between runs of one binary).\n\nValidation: 22/22 Python checksums equal CPython; 17/17 Lua benchmarks equal PUC Lua, with and without ZYLUA_LLVM; compiler lib and linking/OSR/body tests, zyntax_embed, zyntax_python (conformance 50/50) and zyntax_lua (conformance 46/46) suites. resume_points a_region_taking_a_number_aggregate_runs_on_llvm fails as it does at HEAD (2a5cd81).\n\nLanded in 160e0f5f1efe6eab9f81997b93cdf4dbdeb285a5 (inliner) and f3e6f3d3b8efbbd5f02290f5ee76288168ebdb4c (deferral).","files":null}]}